# netsurf: stacking contexts across levels; position: fixed stays put; clicks hit what is on top
#
# MacSurf's CSS 2.1 Appendix E painting only sorted SIBLINGS: a fixed or
# z-indexed element inside a plain wrapper painted in DOM order, under every
# later sibling of the wrapper. That is the usual shape of a modal (Google's
# and YouTube's consent dialogs: div[role=dialog] > div.scrim{fixed;z-index}
# + div.dialog{fixed;z-index}) and of most dropdowns, so dialogs drew under
# the page they cover.
#
# - redraw.c: a positioned box with an explicit z-index, or any fixed box, is
#   "hoisted": its nearest stacking context (the root, or a box the
#   classifier calls one) collects it from its whole subtree and paints it in
#   its z phase (negative before the flow, zero/auto and positive after, in
#   tree order within a z), with the coordinates its parent would have given
#   it; its non-stacking-context parent skips it. Growable vector, no caps.
# - position: fixed boxes are painted vb_fixed_scroll_x/y (the viewport
#   scroll, set by the frontend: vita/window.c) further on, so they stay in
#   the viewport instead of scrolling away with the page.
# - box_inspect.c / interaction.c: hit testing finds the topmost hoisted box
#   under the point (highest z, then last in tree order, nested ones
#   preferred, fixed offset included) and walks only its subtree
#   (box_walk_limit); with none, the walk skips hoisted subtrees
#   (box_walk_skip_hoisted). Before, page content after a modal in the DOM
#   won clicks aimed at the modal.
